\citet{held2024polariton} demonstrated efficient electrical pumping of exciton-polaritons in SWCNT microcavities at room temperature with high current densities and continuous tunability.

\subsection{Carbon Nanotube Phonon Conductance}

Carbon nanotubes are exceptional phonon conductors. The one-dimensional geometry of the nanotube constrains phonon propagation along the tube axis, producing ballistic phonon transport at room temperature. The carbon nanotube lattice therefore does not merely confine the polariton condensate optically --- it participates in sustaining the phonon component of the polariton quasiparticle along the propagation length.

\subsection{Polariton Propagation in One-Dimensional Waveguide Geometries}

\citet{ardizzone2022polariton} demonstrated Bose-Einstein condensation of polaritons in a bound state in the continuum within a patterned waveguide, with tight confinement of the waveguide geometry enabling extremely low threshold condensation density.

The transition from propagating polariton solitons to standing wave polariton condensate in multimode microwire systems \citep{sich2018transition} demonstrates that polariton condensate behavior within a one-dimensional waveguide can be controlled through excitation density --- providing the physical basis for adjustable blade length through coil drive power modulation.

% ============================================================
\section{Proposed Device Architecture}
% ============================================================

\subsection{Overview}

The proposed device consists of five functional components. The crystal substrate generates the polariton condensate through phonon-photon coupling driven by the initiation coil. The polariton condensate propagates from the crystal emitter face into the carbon nanotube lattice blade waveguide. The hilt assembly provides structural housing, power delivery, and operator interface.

\subsection{Crystal Substrate}

The crystal substrate is a single-crystal CsPbBr$_3$ perovskite fabricated in a geometry optimized for polariton condensation and directional emission toward the blade waveguide. The crystal lattice geometry determines the phonon-photon coupling frequency and therefore the blade color through the spectral position of the polariton condensate emission. The emitter face geometry shapes the spatial mode of the condensate exiting the crystal into the blade waveguide.

The crystal and its emitter geometry are a single continuous structure. No material discontinuity is introduced between the condensate generation region and the waveguide coupling point. Any such discontinuity would constitute a decoherence interface.

\subsection{Initiation Coil}

The initiation coil is a solenoid wound around the crystal body within the hilt. It generates an oscillating electromagnetic field at the phonon resonance frequency of the specific crystal substrate, initiating the phonon cascade that drives polariton condensation within the crystal.

The coil must be tuned to the specific crystal. The phonon resonance frequency depends on the specific crystal composition, geometry, and crystalline quality, and varies between crystal samples. This individual coil-crystal relationship is a physical necessity arising from the architecture.

\subsection{Blade Waveguide}

The blade waveguide is a cylindrical lattice structure of single-walled carbon nanotube composite extending from the crystal emitter to the blade tip. The inner diameter is matched to the spatial mode of the polariton condensate emitted from the crystal emitter face.

The lattice geometry provides the photonic confinement required to maintain polariton coherence along the blade length. The phonon conductance of the carbon nanotube material sustains the phonon component of the polariton along the propagation length. The lattice is sufficiently translucent that the optical emission of the polariton condensate is visible as the blade glow.

When the device is active, the polariton condensate pressurizes the lattice structure from within, producing structural rigidity. When the device is deactivated, the lattice returns to its unloaded flexible state. This emergent rigidity behavior is a theoretical prediction that requires experimental demonstration at proof-of-concept scale.

\subsection{Physical Blade Interaction}

Two active blades in contact will produce physical interaction forces through polariton-polariton scattering between the two condensates at the contact region. The effective mass of the polariton ensures that this interaction produces mechanically detectable force. The magnitude of the interaction force is proportional to the polariton density at the contact region, which is controlled by coil drive power.

