\section{Introduction}
% ============================================================

The lightsaber, as depicted in the Star Wars franchise created by George Lucas, presents three physical properties that distinguish it from conventional directed energy devices: a blade of fixed and controllable length, physical interaction between two blades, and a blade that is clearly energetic rather than purely mechanical in nature.

These properties have been treated as requiring physics that does not exist. Specifically, the objection is that photons --- the obvious candidate for an energy blade --- do not interact with each other, do not terminate at a fixed length, and cannot be confined without a physical medium. These objections are correct as applied to photons.

They are not correct as applied to polaritons.

A polariton is a quasiparticle arising from the strong coupling of an exciton and a photon within a cavity \citep{kavokin2007microcavities}. It is a half-light half-matter excitation. Its photonic component gives it coherent optical properties. Its excitonic component gives it effective mass and the capacity for polariton-polariton interaction.

A polariton condensate is a macroscopic quantum state in which polaritons undergo Bose-Einstein condensation --- occupying a single quantum state collectively and exhibiting long-range coherence \citep{kasprzak2006boseeinstein}. This condensate can propagate through a suitable waveguide structure while maintaining its quantum coherent properties.

We propose that the lightsaber blade is a polariton condensate propagating through a carbon nanotube composite lattice waveguide. The fixed blade length is determined by the coherence length of the condensate within the waveguide geometry. The physical interaction between blades is the result of polariton-polariton scattering between two condensates. The visible blade is the optical emission of the condensate through the translucent nanotube lattice.

