% ============================================================
\section{Engineering Challenges and Proof of Concept}
% ============================================================

\subsection{Primary Engineering Challenges}

\textbf{Challenge 1: Crystal synthesis at required optical quality.} Nonradiative recombination sites within the crystal reduce polariton lifetime and shorten coherence length. Template-assisted synthesis methods are the current state of the art \citep{kedziorowa2024predesigned}.

\textbf{Challenge 2: Emitter face nanofabrication.} Shaping the crystal emitter face to the required geometry requires focused ion beam milling or equivalent nanofabrication access.

\textbf{Challenge 3: Blade waveguide scaling.} Carbon nanotube polariton waveguide demonstration at centimeter scale is achievable with current technology. Scaling to blade length requires manufacturing development not currently demonstrated.

\textbf{Challenge 4: Condensate-waveguide rigidity.} The structural rigidity of the blade under active condensate operation is a theoretically predicted emergent property that has not been demonstrated experimentally at any scale.

\textbf{Challenge 5: Coil tuning methodology.} A standardized procedure for measuring crystal phonon resonance frequency post-synthesis and winding a matched coil requires development.

\subsection{Minimum Viable Proof-of-Concept Experiment}

\textbf{Goal:} Demonstrate polariton condensate propagation through a carbon nanotube composite waveguide at room temperature, initiated by electromagnetic coil, with measurable coherence at the waveguide output.

\textbf{Required equipment:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item CsPbBr$_3$ single crystal perovskite microwire
    \item Single-walled carbon nanotube composite film on cylindrical substrate, 1~cm length
    \item Custom wound solenoid coil tuned to crystal resonance frequency
    \item Continuous wave drive source at calibrated frequency
    \item Photoluminescence spectroscopy setup
    \item Interferometric measurement for coherence length characterization
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Success criteria:} Photoluminescence spectroscopy confirms polariton condensation above threshold at the crystal. Interferometric measurement confirms coherence propagation through the nanotube waveguide beyond the crystal boundary. Condensate emission is detectable at the waveguide output end.

This experiment is achievable with university laboratory access at standard condensed matter optics facilities.

% ============================================================
\section{Theoretical Extensions and Open Questions}
% ============================================================

\textbf{Blade length control mechanism.} The proposed control of blade length through coil drive power modulation is theoretically supported by the density-coherence relationship in polariton condensates \citep{sich2018transition}. The precise transfer function between coil power and coherence length in the proposed geometry requires experimental characterization.

\textbf{Blade color determination.} The color of the blade is predicted to be determined by the spectral position of the polariton condensate emission, set by the crystal lattice geometry and composition. This prediction is directly testable.

\textbf{Condensate-waveguide mechanical coupling.} The predicted structural rigidity of the blade under active condensate operation is the most theoretically novel claim in this paper. The mechanism proposed is polariton radiation pressure within the confined waveguide geometry. Quantitative modeling of this effect is required.

\textbf{Power delivery architecture.} The power requirements for continuous-wave operation at demonstrated perovskite condensation thresholds are compatible with compact battery technology \citep{wu2025continuous}. A full power system design requires prototype measurement of actual coil drive requirements for the specific crystal geometry.

% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}
% ============================================================

The lightsaber blade as a polariton condensate propagating through a carbon nanotube composite waveguide is not a speculative proposal requiring undiscovered physics. It is an engineering proposal requiring the integration of physics that has been separately confirmed in multiple experimental contexts.

Room temperature polariton condensation in perovskite crystals is confirmed. Polariton propagation through carbon nanotube structures is confirmed. Polariton-polariton interaction as the basis for blade-on-blade physical contact is grounded in confirmed polariton physics.

The obstacles between the current state of the art and a functional device are fabrication challenges: crystal quality, emitter geometry nanofabrication, blade waveguide scaling, and the undemonstrated condensate-waveguide mechanical coupling. These are engineering problems, not physics problems.

George Lucas imagined a device whose physical realization was not available in 1977. It is available now. The question is whether anyone will build it.

