    \begin{abstract}
    \noindent
    The lightsaber --- a blade of coherent energy capable of physical interaction with another blade --- has been treated as fictional technology requiring physics that does not yet exist. This paper argues the contrary position: that the necessary physics already exists, and that the obstacle is engineering rather than fundamental science. We propose that the lightsaber blade is realizable as a polariton condensate propagating through a carbon nanotube composite waveguide, initiated by an electromagnetic coil tuned to a perovskite crystal substrate. Each component of this architecture is grounded in published experimental results. Polariton condensation in perovskite crystals at room temperature is confirmed, including continuous-wave operation at low threshold power densities. Polariton propagation through single-walled carbon nanotube structures has been demonstrated. The physical interaction between polariton blades is theoretically supported by the non-zero effective mass of polaritons and demonstrated polariton-polariton scattering behavior. We identify five core engineering challenges that separate current laboratory capability from a functional device, propose a minimum viable proof-of-concept experiment, and outline a theoretical build specification for the complete device architecture. This paper does not claim to have built a lightsaber. It claims that building one is a materials science and fabrication problem, not a physics problem.
    \end{abstract}

