Polaritons are bosonic quasiparticles. Above a critical density, they can undergo Bose-Einstein condensation into the lowest energy state, exhibiting macroscopic phase coherence, stimulated scattering, and superfluid behavior \citep{carusotto2013quantum}. The resulting polariton condensate has a coherence length determined by the cavity geometry, the condensate density, and the polariton lifetime.

The effective mass of a polariton is typically four to five orders of magnitude smaller than the electron mass. This low effective mass allows condensation at temperatures far exceeding those required for atomic Bose-Einstein condensates. The excitonic component provides the nonlinearity --- the polariton-polariton interaction --- essential for both condensate formation and blade-on-blade interaction.

